meta {
  name: ListGroepNoMatchNoArtnr
  type: http
  seq: 21
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/web/artikel?groep=ZZZ_NONEXISTENT_GROEP
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("responds in under 5s - was reproduced at 13.08-13.09s live before the fix", function() {
    expect(res.getResponseTime()).to.be.below(5000);
  });
}

docs {
  Regression test for a backend-tester-confirmed latency bug:
  GET /web/artikel?groep=<value> with NO artnr filter took 13+s live
  (measured twice: 13084ms and 13094ms), because "artikel.groep EQ
  pcGroep" still lived directly in the FOR EACH ... WHERE clause in
  Luna.BusinessLogic.ArtikelBE:GetArtikelen(). When pcArtnr is blank
  (no index-range narrowing), a WHERE-clause condition on groep is
  evaluated by the query engine BEFORE a row ever reaches the loop
  body, so non-matching rows never incremented iRowsScanned and the
  15,000-row scan-depth cap (added earlier for the analogous
  omschrijving bug, see ListOmschrijvingEncodedEquals.bru) never
  triggered - the engine silently ground through the full ~1.8M-row
  table underneath it.

  Fixed the same way as omschrijving: "groep EQ pcGroep" moved out of
  the WHERE clause into a per-row "IF pcGroep NE '' AND artikel.groep
  NE pcGroep THEN NEXT." check inside the loop body, so every
  physically-visited row counts towards the cap regardless of match.
  The artnr BEGINS condition is untouched and still drives the index
  range when artnr is supplied. Measured live afterwards at
  ~0.6-0.7s for this exact request.

  See ListOmschrijvingEncodedEquals.bru for the original, analogous
  fix this one mirrors, and List.bru / the ArtikelBE class header for
  the groep+artnr combined-filter behaviour (artnr still narrows the
  index range; groep is evaluated per-row on top of that range).
}